    ("NodeAsAnim", 'Node', "anim", (("node", "Node"), ),
     "The same scene node as a MaxMcpAnim, because INode IS a ReferenceTarget (include/inode.h:55) and every graph entry here takes an anim. Identity translation, which is the facade's own job - the two handle spaces exist because a material has no INode, not because the objects are different objects. The mint is Animatable::GetHandleByAnim (include/Animatable.h:282), and its own contract is worth passing on (:274-279): handles are unique within a scene, are NOT saved with the scene, and are never reused after a delete, so a stale one resolves to nothing rather than to somebody else. ACCEPTS MAXMCP_FACADE_ROOT_NODE: the root is a ReferenceMaker and turns up in reference and dependent walks. MAXMCP_FACADE_NO_ANIM for a handle that does not resolve.", 'the sub-anim and reference graph, ABI 18'),
    ("AnimAsNode", 'Anim', "node", (("anim", "Anim"), ),
     "The other direction - the dependents of an object are mostly nodes, so this is what makes AnimDependents usable. MAXMCP_FACADE_NO_NODE for anything that is not a node, decided by SuperClassID() == BASENODE_CLASS_ID (include/plugapi.h:406). That define sits in a group its own header marks reserved and internal (include/plugapi.h:398-401), and we use it anyway because 3ds Max's own plug-ins do - samples/controllers/exprctrl.cpp:1150, samples/controllers/block/driverblock.cpp:2399 and samples/CAT/src/CATControls/CATNodeControl.cpp:3223 are the same comparison, and the last two make it inside a DependentEnumProc, which is this exact walk. Written down so it is a decision and not an oversight. The scene ROOT answers MAXMCP_FACADE_ROOT_NODE: its ordinary handle is kNullHandle (include/inode.h:60), which is MAXMCP_FACADE_NO_NODE, so a bridge that minted it the ordinary way would report 'no node' for the one node every walk ends at. TWO FACTS ARRIVE AT MAXMCP_FACADE_NO_NODE HERE - the handle did not resolve, and it resolved to something that is not a node - and they are NOT split, deliberately: every shipped handle-returning entry folds them the same way (NodeTMController, ControllerSubController), and one bridge answering differently from every edge read around it is worse than the fold. A caller that needs them apart asks AnimClassId first, which refuses a dead handle and answers a live one.", 'the sub-anim and reference graph, ABI 18'),
